<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auguste de Marmont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Ragusa, Auguste Frédéric Louis Viesse de Marmont</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auguste de Marmont was a French general and Marshal of the Empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. As commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> he abolished the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a> in 1807 and the Republic of Ragusa in 1808, receiving the title Duke of Ragusa, and governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> from 1809 to 1811. His surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814 was long seen as betrayal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont.jpg" alt="Auguste de Marmont" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Appiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auguste_de_Marmont.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Auguste Frédéric Louis Viesse de Marmont was born on 20 July 1774 at Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, the son of a former army officer of the petty nobility who adopted the principles of the Revolution. He entered the artillery and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, whom he came to know better when he served at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>. He became General Bonaparte's aide-de-camp, accompanied him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and was promoted general of brigade. For commanding the artillery at Marengo he was made general of division.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1805 Marmont commanded a corps at Ulm and was then sent to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and occupy the Republic of Ragusa. For five years he was military and civil governor of Dalmatia. In 1809 he defeated an Austrian force there, defeated Ignaz Gyulai's corps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> on 5 and 6 July. Napoleon made him a Marshal of the Empire, saying that he had not done enough to justify the choice entirely, and appointed him governor-general of the Illyrian provinces.</p><p>In May 1811 he was summoned to succeed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">André Masséna</a> in command of the French army in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. At the Battle of Salamanca Wellington's cavalry charged his left flank and inflicted a great defeat, and Marmont was gravely wounded early in the battle. In 1813 he led a corps at Lützen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, and he fought through the defensive campaign of 1814.</p><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814, believing that prolonging the war was pointless, he reached a secret agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. Together with marshals Mortier and Moncey he marched to a position where Allied troops surrounded them and they surrendered. The verb raguser, from his title, became a French word meaning to betray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marmont stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>. As major-general of the Royal Guard he led the royalist troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830, but gave up when it became clear that they were outmatched. Charles X ordered him arrested, asking whether he would betray them as he had betrayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. He went into exile, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where he was tutor to the Duke of Reichstadt, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 22 March 1852, the last living Napoleonic marshal. His Mémoires, published after his death, are of great value for the military history of the period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auguste de Marmont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 31 January 1808 Marshal <b>Auguste Marmont</b> proclaimed the abolition of the republic, and its territory was soon incorporated into the Illyrian Provinces of the French Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A governor-general in Ljubljana, first Marshal <b>Auguste de Marmont</b>, ruled under French law, abolishing many feudal dues, building roads and opening schools teaching in Slovene and Croatian.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>When Poljica men joined a Russian-backed rising, <b>Marshal Marmont</b> abolished the republic in 1807 and merged it into French Dalmatia, later the Illyrian Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-poljica">Republic of Poljica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Châtillon-sur-Seine, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.86,4.57&amp;year=1824" aria-label="Châtillon-sur-Seine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Auguste de Marmont"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 6022h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 6022h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.86,4.57&amp;year=1824">What was here in 1824? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
